summary:
- topic: FY2026 Budget Proposal
  text: The Superintendent presented the FY2026 budget, which requests a $3.17 million
    increase from taxpayers for a total school operating budget increase of just under
    6%, despite significant proposed reductions.
- topic: Enrollment and Staffing Trends
  text: A primary justification for cuts is a significant and ongoing decline in student
    enrollment, projected to be the lowest in over 15 years, while overall staffing
    has increased by 88 positions since 2017.
- topic: Personnel Reductions
  text: The budget proposes numerous staff reductions, including the Director of Curriculum,
    Instruction, and Assessment, ESOL teachers, Social Emotional Learning (SEL) teachers,
    a McKinney-Vento social worker, and several Ed Tech positions.
- topic: Taxpayer Impact
  text: The Superintendent highlighted the significant tax burden on residential property
    owners, noting an average 38% increase in school taxes for the average single-family
    home over the past year, largely due to property revaluations.
- topic: Preserved Positions & Program Cuts
  text: The district plans to absorb 11.5 teaching positions (including special education
    and literacy specialists) into the local budget to save them, but will eliminate
    the $150,000 second lunch program to reduce costs.
- topic: Public Comment Highlights
  text: Staff, parents, and students voiced strong opposition to cuts affecting student-facing
    roles, particularly the Director of Curriculum, SEL teachers, and the percussion
    Ed Tech, while a resident spoke on the severe impact of rising taxes on fixed-income
    homeowners.
- topic: Future Initiatives
  text: The Superintendent announced plans to establish a South Portland Education
    Foundation for fundraising and confirmed a scaled-back athletic complex renovation
    bond will go to voters in November, with no budget impact until FY2027.
